<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pushyamitra Shunga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. c. 185–149 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pushyamitra Shunga was the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunga-empire">Shunga dynasty</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Commander of the Maurya army, he assassinated the last Maurya emperor, Brihadratha, around 185 BCE and took the throne at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pataliputra">Pataliputra</a>. He performed Vedic horse sacrifices, marking a revival of Brahmanical kingship, and Buddhist texts accuse him of persecution, though Buddhist art flourished in his dynasty's time.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga.jpg" alt="Pushyamitra Shunga" width="320" height="336" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Leiden University Library / Royal Netherlands Institute of Southeast Asian and Caribbean Studies · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bharhut_Sunga_individual.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Demetrius I is generally considered the Greco-Bactrian king who first launched the Greek expansion into India, around 200 BCE, and so the founder of the Indo-Greek realm; the overthrow of the Mauryas by <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b> about 185 BCE is thought to have encouraged it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indo-greek-kingdom">Indo-Greek Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 185 BCE the last Maurya emperor, Brihadratha, was assassinated by his commander-in-chief, <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b>, who founded the Shunga dynasty in the Gangetic heartland, marking the end of Mauryan rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 185 BCE the Brahmin general <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b> killed the last Maurya king and founded his own dynasty, and the Gupta records never name the family’s varna.</blockquote><a href="/societies/classical-india">Social Hierarchy of Classical India</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 185 BCE <b>Pushyamitra Shunga</b>, commander of the Maurya army, assassinated the last Maurya emperor, Brihadratha, during a military parade and took the throne at Pataliputra.</blockquote><a href="/state/shunga-empire">Shunga Empire</a></li></ul></section>
